{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Updated the event-backpressure question with the executed consequence case. Receipt 76557a280cf8429d8ce3102d7565decb (experiment 2b18b51118e04d8a9f0ca6001d18eb51-experiment, exit 0, all three tests passed) carries everything in one run: event 2 `parked_majority_cluster` {nodes_parked:2, healthy_node_responsive:true, append_notify_timeout_reached:true, decided_before:7, decided_after:7} — only node 1 consumes its event stream (capacity 8), two of three nodes park, and the healthy node reaches the documented Timeout and cannot move the decided index; events 0/1 are the drained control (all three answering, follower append_notify Ok); events 3/4 restate the node-local freeze (bound reached, permanently parked). So the finding now holds at both scopes in a single receipt, the earlier 'cluster-level consequence not measured' limitation is withdrawn, and the remaining limits are the ones actually measured: bounds witnessed are 64 and 8 rather than the 1024 default (capacity-independence argued from source), the trigger is client-side with no documented drain obligation, a dropped receiver unblocks the actor, and the cluster case witnesses one schedule with two of three nodes parked. Unfinished work this turn: the identity probe for a third lead (append_notify resolving Ok for an entry overwritten at that index) is written but currently fails for a harness reason — node B's own append_notify times out instead of deciding — so it was not submitted; next turn debugs it or reselects.\"}"}}
